5. I consider J5 or F9. If Black doesn\'t block, they\'ll lose immediately. I must evaluate where to put that block stone to disrupt the diagonal line.\n\nIf I play K8 (extending my three), White *must* respond to block the 4, and the turn goes to White.\n\nSo I am back to blocking:\n*   F9: Does it block the diagonal? Yes. But it is not a center position.\n*   J5: Does it block the diagonal? Yes. It\'s closer to Black\'s own stones at I7, J8, etc. which might allow me to connect later.\n\nI need to decide: J5 or F9. Is there any way to turn this into an attack?\n*   Blocking at J5, I need to look around to see if I am helping White create more threats.\n*   Blocking at F9, I need to look at if I connect or create an advantage by positioning my stone.\n\nI look for any 4-3 combination. Any way to make a 4 AND a 3. I do not see any in K8, or I10. The only way is to block.\n\nI do not find a way to make a four and create any other threat (or an open 3). It all boils down to blocking the diagonal. Now, the question is *where*?\n\nI’ve assessed that I *must* block to survive. Since J5 breaks up the block and is closer to Black stones. F9 is too far from our stones.\n\nThen it\'s a game of blocking. I have to play defense. And this gets to the key point: *I* must block White\'s diagonal Open 3 (G8-H7-I6) to prevent their win.\n\nHere’s my final assessment:\n\nEvaluation: White has a lethal threat: a diagonal Open 3 (G8-H7-I6). If not blocked, White will play J5 or F9 to form an Open 4, leading to an inevitable win. Black has 3-in-a-row lines (Row 8 and Col I), but they are \'closed\' (blocked on one side), so extending them (e.g., to K8 or I10) only creates a blocked 4, which forces a single defensive move from White but doesn\'t lead to a consecutive winning combination (VCF). Therefore, Black is forced to defend against White\'s immediate threat.\n\nReason: Blocking at J5 is necessary to stop White\'s diagonal Open 3 from becoming a winning Open 4. J5 is preferred over