cally, White has a broken line of three stones on Row 6 (G6, I6, J6) and a solid line of three stones on Column G (G6, G7, G8).\n1.  **Row 6 Threat:** If White plays at **H6**, they will connect G6, I6, and J6 to form a solid line of 4 stones (G6-H6-I6-J6). Since F6 and K6 are empty, this would result in an "Open 4," which guarantees a win for White on the following turn.\n2.  **Column G Threat:** White also has an Open 3 on Column G (G6-G7-G8). While dangerous, this requires White to play G5 or G9 to become an Open 4.\n3.  **Black\'s Strategy:** The threat of an immediate Open 4 on Row 6 is the highest priority defense. Black **must** block at H6. Fortuitously, playing at H6 serves a dual purpose: it blocks White\'s win and simultaneously forms a diagonal "Open 3" for Black (connecting H6, I7, and J8). This counter-threat forces White to calculate defensively, potentially buying Black time or leading to a tactical exchange, whereas other blocking moves (like blocking Column G) would result in an immediate loss to the Row 6 threat.\n\nReason: White threatens an unstoppable win by playing H6 (creating an Open 4). Black must block this coordinate immediately. Furthermore, H6 is an excellent move as it creates a diagonal Open 3 for Black (H6-I7-J8), shifting the initiative.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "H", "row": 6 }'}
